\section{Problem Statement Formulation}

\subsection{High-Level Thematic Problem}

Sand is the second-most exploited natural resource in the world after water. It is the primary input for concrete, glass, asphalt, and electronics manufacturing, making it indispensable to modern construction and industry. The United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP) estimates that more than 50 billion tonnes of sand and gravel are extracted globally every year, a figure that dwarfs the replenishment capacity of rivers and coastal systems~\cite{sand_unep}. Demand is only expected to intensify: the United Nations estimates that by 2030, the world will have 40 megacities (cities with populations exceeding 10 million), compared to 31 today~\cite{sand_pop}, each requiring enormous quantities of construction-grade aggregates. India sits at the center of this pressure: it is one of the fastest-urbanizing nations on earth, with a government-mandated housing program (the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana-Urban, or PMAY-U) that has sanctioned nearly 12 million new houses since its launch in 2015.

Because legally permitted sand extraction struggles to meet this accelerating demand, a shadow supply chain has emerged. India's ``Sand Mafia'' is an informal network of organized criminal groups that extract, transport, and sell sand illegally from rivers, floodplains, and coastal areas. Their operations are frequently accompanied by violence: between 2019 and 2023, documented incidents include the deaths of government officials, journalists, and civilians who attempted to report or obstruct mining activities~\cite{sandwatch_court}. Despite the scale and severity of this phenomenon, academic and policy research on India's Sand Mafia remains sparse. Most existing literature treats it as a localized governance failure rather than a systemic, nationally distributed organized crime problem with measurable structural drivers.

This report attempts to partially fill that gap. We treat illegal sand mining not as an isolated criminal event but as a complex socioeconomic outcome shaped by construction demand, poverty, enforcement capacity, environmental conditions, and geographic opportunity. Our analysis proceeds through four thematic lenses:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Geographic lens:} Where is illegal sand mining concentrated, and does it form spatial clusters that suggest coordinated criminal networks rather than opportunistic individual actors?
    \item \textbf{Demand lens:} Can we demonstrate a causal link between large-scale government construction programs and the intensification of illegal extraction?
    \item \textbf{Socioeconomic lens:} Are states with higher rates of poverty, marginal employment, and poor housing conditions more prone to sustained illegal mining activity?
    \item \textbf{Environmental lens:} Does proximity to active sand mining sites correlate with measurable degradation in groundwater quality and air particulate levels?
\end{enumerate}

Our end goal is not merely descriptive. We aim to identify which policy levers (enforcement, legal reform, environmental monitoring, and alternative livelihoods) are most likely to disrupt illegal mining operations, and to present this analysis in a form that can directly inform government action.

\subsection{Why Sand Mining Is Harmful}

Before proceeding to the data, it is worth grounding the problem in its environmental and human consequences, because the urgency of these consequences is what justifies the analytical effort.

\textbf{Ecological destruction.} Rivers are not static containers of water. They are dynamic systems in which sand and gravel serve as habitat for thousands of species, regulate streamflow, anchor riverbanks, and filter groundwater. When sand is removed from riverbeds faster than natural deposition can replace it (a process called \textit{riverbed incision}), channels deepen and banks become unstable. This triggers a cascade: bridges lose their foundations, floodplains no longer receive seasonal sediment, and the water table drops because the natural sponge layer beneath the river is removed. Studies of heavily mined rivers in India have documented bank collapses, loss of aquatic biodiversity, and the drying up of small tributaries~\cite{dharmadhikary2018}.

\textbf{Groundwater contamination.} Sand mining disturbs the mineral-rich sediment layer of riverbeds, releasing dissolved solids (including heavy metals) into the water column. Total Dissolved Solids (TDS), the measure of the combined content of all inorganic and organic substances dissolved in water, are a standard proxy for this contamination. Our analysis of Water Resources Information System (WRIS) groundwater quality data across India, examined in Section~\ref{sec:spatial}, finds that stations closer to active mining sites tend to show elevated TDS readings, with the effect most pronounced in Bihar and West Bengal.

\textbf{Air quality degradation.} The transport of sand by truck, tractor, and barge generates large quantities of coarse particulate matter. PM10 and PM2.5, the two standard regulatory metrics for airborne particles (referring to particles with diameters of 10 and 2.5 micrometers or less, respectively), have been linked to respiratory disease, cardiovascular damage, and premature death in exposed populations. Our spatial regression analysis finds that PM10 is the most strongly correlated environmental variable with mining intensity at the state level.

\textbf{Violence and impunity.} The Sand Mafia operates in a context of systemic legal impunity. Court documents in our dataset reveal patterns in which local officials are allegedly bribed, whistleblowers are threatened, and environmental regulations are routinely violated without sanction. The gap between observed mining intensity and the rate of court-recognized legal cases (analyzed in Section~\ref{sec:classification}) is itself a measure of this impunity.

\textbf{Social inequality.} Illegal sand mining is not uniformly distributed across society. It correlates highly with the geographic concentration of marginal workers in river-adjacent and resource-dependent areas, where livelihoods are most vulnerable to environmental disruption. The communities whose rivers and groundwater are depleted receive no compensation. This makes illegal sand mining both an environmental problem and a question of social justice.

\subsection{The Data Integration Challenge}
\label{sec:data_challenge}

The central methodological challenge of this project is one that no amount of analytical sophistication can fully overcome: \textit{there is no single, authoritative, comprehensive dataset on illegal sand mining in India.} Unlike conventional crime statistics, which are collected by a centralized agency (the NCRB) and published in a standardized annual format, sand mining incidents are scattered across field observation networks, journalistic archives, court filings, government district surveys, and environmental monitoring databases that were never designed to interoperate.

This fragmentation is not accidental. The Sand Mafia thrives in informational darkness: the absence of systematic documentation is itself a governance failure that perpetuates impunity. When there is no official count of mining incidents, there is no official accountability for failing to address them.

Our response to this challenge is the defining accomplishment of this project: the assembly of a unified analytical framework from seven fundamentally different data sources.

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{India Sand Watch (Veditum India Foundation):} 2,557 incident records from field observers, journalists, court documents, and government district surveys, spanning 2001 to 2026. This is the only publicly available national-scale mining incident database in India. Its coverage is uneven (Bihar and Madhya Pradesh are overrepresented; northeastern states are nearly absent), and its records are heterogeneous in quality and completeness.
    \item \textbf{National Data and Analytics Platform (NDAP):} Official government statistical series from the NCRB (crime and police personnel), Census 2011 (population, literacy, marginal workers, household conditions), CPCB (air quality), and PMAY-U (housing construction progress and central assistance allocations).
    \item \textbf{Water Resources Information System (WRIS):} 7,190 groundwater monitoring station records with location coordinates and Total Dissolved Solids (TDS) measurements, spatially linked to the nearest confirmed mining point for Geographically Weighted Regression.
    \item \textbf{Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB):} State-level annual average PM2.5 and PM10 concentrations from the national ambient air quality monitoring network.
    \item \textbf{PMAY-U Administrative Records:} State-level housing sanctions, groundings, completions, and central government assistance allocations, used as the primary treatment variable in the causal analyses.
    \item \textbf{Census 2011 Socioeconomic Tables:} State-level population, literacy rates, marginal worker proportions, and household condition data (good, liveable, dilapidated), used as socioeconomic covariates.
    \item \textbf{Prayagraj Water Quality Sensors:} Two continuous monitoring buoys capturing approximately 99,000 one-minute readings of dissolved oxygen, pH, oxidation-reduction potential, conductivity, temperature, and a composite Water Quality Index (WQI) between January 2019 and February 2020.
\end{enumerate}

Each of these datasets uses different geographic granularities (some state-level, some district-level, some point-level), different temporal resolutions (annual, monthly, daily, per-minute), different state name conventions, and different units and measurement standards. A substantial portion of our technical effort was devoted to making these datasets speak to each other at all, including a systematic Unicode normalization pipeline, a manually curated state name correction dictionary, and spatial join procedures linking point-level sensor and mining data to administrative polygon boundaries.

The result is not a perfect dataset. It is the most complete picture of illegal sand mining in India that can currently be assembled from publicly available sources, and demonstrating that it is possible to derive meaningful systemic analysis from these fragments is a contribution to future research in this area.

\subsection{Primary Relevant Datasets}

Our dataset is assembled from two primary sources: the India Sand Watch platform maintained by Veditum India Foundation, and the National Data and Analytics Platform (NDAP) operated by the Government of India.

\subsubsection{NDAP Datasets Utilized}

The National Data and Analytics Platform aggregates official government statistical series from multiple ministries and agencies. The following NDAP datasets were used in this analysis:

\textbf{Crime in India (NCRB).} The National Crime Records Bureau (NCRB) publishes annual state-level crime statistics under the Indian Penal Code (IPC). We use the ``cognizable crimes reported'' rate per 100,000 population as a proxy for the overall law enforcement environment in each state. A cognizable crime is one for which a police officer may arrest without a warrant under Indian law. The dataset covers 2022 data across all 36 states and Union Territories.

\textbf{Police Personnel (NCRB).} State-level data on the total number of authorized and actual police personnel, disaggregated by rank (constable, sub-inspector, inspector, etc.). We aggregate these into a single ``total police strength'' variable and compute a mining-incidents-per-police-personnel ratio to assess enforcement capacity relative to mining activity.

\textbf{Household Conditions (Census).} State-level data on the proportion of households classified as being in good, liveable, or dilapidated condition. This serves as a proxy for material poverty and unmet housing needs.

\textbf{Population and Literacy (Census 2011).} State-level data on total population, literate population (used to compute literacy rate), and the proportion of ``marginal workers'' (a Census category referring to individuals who work fewer than 183 days per year, indicating unstable, seasonal, or informal employment).

\textbf{PM2.5 and PM10 Air Quality (CPCB).} Annual average concentrations of fine and coarse particulate matter measured at state-level monitoring stations, compiled from the Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB) database.

\textbf{Groundwater Quality (WRIS).} The Water Resources Information System maintains a national network of groundwater monitoring stations. We use station-level latitude, longitude, and Total Dissolved Solids (TDS) measurements, aggregated by state and by individual station for the Geographically Weighted Regression analysis described in Section~\ref{sec:spatial}.

\textbf{PMAY-U Construction Data.} State-level and district-level data on houses sanctioned, grounded, and completed under the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana-Urban scheme, as well as the central government assistance allocation (in crore rupees) to each state. This is the primary measure of construction demand in our causal analysis.

\subsubsection{External Datasets Utilized}

\textbf{India Sand Watch (Veditum).} This is our primary source for mining incidents. It contains four sub-datasets:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textit{Mining observations} (375 records): Field reports submitted by citizen observers, journalists, and researchers, containing the location, date, type of machinery used, and descriptive notes for each observed mining incident.
    \item \textit{Court documents} (91 records): Legal filings and judgments from the National Green Tribunal (NGT), High Courts, and the Supreme Court of India related to sand mining cases, with metadata including the state, document type, and a text summary.
    \item \textit{News reports} (124 records): Media articles about sand mining incidents, organized by state.
    \item \textit{District survey reports} (91 records): Official government district surveys mandated under the Minor Minerals Rules, containing structured assessments of sand availability and extraction in each district.
    \item \textit{Unified geo data} (2,212 verified records): A consolidated dataset of all mining incidents with latitude and longitude coordinates, combining the above sources with additional records.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Ganga and Sangam water quality sensors.} Two continuous water quality monitoring buoys deployed at Prayagraj, Uttar Pradesh, capturing approximately 46,528 and 52,363 one-minute readings respectively between January 2019 and February 2020. Variables include Dissolved Oxygen (DO), pH, Oxidation-Reduction Potential (ORP), conductivity (in microsiemens per centimeter), temperature, and a composite Water Quality Index (WQI).

\textbf{India administrative GeoJSON files.} State and district-level polygon shapefiles from the GADM global administrative areas database, used as the geographic backbone for all choropleth maps, spatial weight matrices, and spatial regression models.

\subsection{Exploratory Data Analysis Results}

\subsubsection{Mining Observations}

The 375 field observation records span 2016 to 2023, with the majority concentrated after 2017. Bihar is the single most-reported state with 193 observations, followed by Uttar Pradesh (48), West Bengal (48), and Madhya Pradesh (43). This distribution is notably skewed: Bihar alone accounts for 51\% of all field observations. However, the broader unified geo dataset (2,212 records) tells a different story at the state level: Madhya Pradesh leads with 369 incidents, followed by Maharashtra (210), Uttar Pradesh (130), and Tamil Nadu (125). The divergence between observation data and aggregated incident data reflects differences in data collection methodology: field observers are concentrated in certain regions, while the aggregated data incorporates news reports, court documents, and survey data that are more uniformly distributed.

A key early finding is the severe mismatch between states in terms of ``formal'' versus ``informal'' reporting. Bihar has 193 informal field observations but very few corresponding court cases. Madhya Pradesh, by contrast, has 279 news-level incidents and a substantial body of NGT court filings. This asymmetry hints at differential legal enforcement, and motivates the classification analysis in Section~\ref{sec:classification}.

