\subsubsection{Court Documents}

The 91 court documents span cases from the Supreme Court, various High Courts, and the National Green Tribunal. Topic modeling (using Latent Dirichlet Allocation, a technique that identifies recurring thematic groups of words in a collection of documents) on the Madhya Pradesh subset identified five dominant topic clusters: (1) illegal mining and environmental clearance violations, (2) Sand Mining Rules and Minerals Act provisions, (3) NGT orders and committee reports, (4) district-level enforcement actions, and (5) applications for lease renewals. Pattern matching using regular expressions (pattern-matching rules applied to text) across all 91 documents found that 63 contained explicit references to ``illegal mining,'' 26 referenced a failure to obtain environmental clearance, and 22 cited environmental violations; collectively these patterns suggest that the legal system is primarily engaged with environmental rather than criminal dimensions of the problem.

\subsubsection{Temporal Trends}

Mining incidents in the unified dataset show a dramatic and statistically significant increase beginning in 2015, the year PMAY-U was launched. Pre-2015 incidents average 13 per year; post-2015 incidents average 191 per year: a 14.7$\times$ increase. This temporal break is the starting point for the causal analysis in Section~\ref{sec:construction}.

\section{Database System}

\subsection{Storing the Data}

Given the diverse and multi-format nature of our data sources (CSV files from NDAP, GeoJSON shapefiles, CSV exports from India Sand Watch, and raw sensor logs), we adopted a hybrid storage architecture appropriate for a research-scale project.

The primary relational store is a PostgreSQL database hosted on Supabase, a cloud-hosted open-source database platform. All mining observation, court document, news report, and district survey data are stored in a unified table (\texttt{no\_mobs\_unified\_geo\_data}) with standardized column schemas. Geographic data (state and district polygon shapefiles) are managed as GeoJSON files read directly into Python via the GeoPandas library (an extension of the Pandas data analysis library that can handle geographic geometries). Environmental datasets (air quality, groundwater quality, sensor data) are stored as flat CSV files and joined on normalized state names at query time.

A key challenge throughout this project was state name normalization. Official government datasets, India Sand Watch records, and geographic shapefiles use inconsistent spellings and abbreviations for the same administrative unit (for example, ``Andaman \& Nicobar,'' ``Andaman and Nicobar Islands,'' and ``Andaman Nicobar''). We implemented a systematic normalization pipeline using Unicode normalization (to remove diacritics), lowercase conversion, punctuation stripping, and a manually curated correction dictionary mapping known variant spellings to a canonical form. This pipeline was applied uniformly across all datasets before any merge operation.

For the spatial dashboard handoff (documented in \texttt{dashboard\_handoff\_resource.ipynb}), all analysis outputs were serialized as GeoJSON layer files and a JSON manifest, providing us with a well-defined interface independent of the analysis codebase.

\subsection{Analysis}

Our analysis is organized into five interconnected modules, each corresponding to a specific notebook and a specific analytical question.

\subsubsection{Spatial Distribution and Clustering (spatial\_analysis.ipynb)}
\label{sec:spatial}

\textbf{Kernel Density Estimation.} We first constructed a continuous spatial density surface over India using Kernel Density Estimation (KDE). KDE is a non-parametric statistical technique that estimates the probability density of a variable (in this case, mining incidents) at each point in a geographic area, by placing a smooth ``kernel'' (a mathematically defined bump) at each observed data point and summing the overlapping kernels. Unlike a simple dot map, KDE produces a continuous surface that reveals where events are most densely concentrated.

Using 373 geo-coded mining points from the unified dataset (the subset with valid latitude and longitude coordinates), KDE with a bandwidth of 0.08 degrees identifies two distinct concentration corridors:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{The Gangetic Plains corridor:} A high-density zone stretching along the Ganga river and its tributaries through Bihar, eastern Uttar Pradesh, and West Bengal. In Bihar alone, the Ganga river accounts for 142 of 268 total records, with the Patna district contributing 105 records: the single hottest geographic concentration in the country.
    \item \textbf{The Central India Plateau corridor:} A high-density zone across Madhya Pradesh and Rajasthan, driven primarily by mining on the Chambal and Narmada rivers. Morena district in Madhya Pradesh has the highest district-level count nationally at 81 records, reflecting the Chambal's relatively remote and politically sensitive geography.
\end{enumerate}

We also constructed a ``mining-to-monitoring ratio'' map comparing mining incident density against groundwater monitoring station density. This map reveals that northeastern districts of India have high mining incident density but relatively low monitoring station coverage, identifying probable under-detection zones where the true scale of illegal extraction likely exceeds what the data capture.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_kde_maps.png}
\caption{KDE intensity surfaces over India. Left: confirmed mining site density (bandwidth $= 0.08^\circ$), showing two concentration corridors along the Gangetic plains and the Central India plateau. Center: groundwater monitoring station density. Right: mining-to-monitoring ratio, identifying northeastern districts as probable under-detection zones where illegal activity outpaces observational coverage.}
\label{fig:kde}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Global Moran's I.} To test whether the spatial distribution of mining is random or exhibits geographic clustering, we computed Moran's I, a measure of spatial autocorrelation ranging from $-1$ (perfect dispersion) through $0$ (complete spatial randomness) to $+1$ (perfect clustering). We defined spatial neighbors using a Queen contiguity weights matrix, meaning that two states are considered neighbors if they share at least a single boundary point. The weights matrix is row-standardized so that the weights of each state's neighbors sum to one.

The global Moran's I for state-level mining counts is $I = 0.121$ ($z = 1.29$, $p = 0.108$ under 999 permutations). This represents a marginal positive clustering tendency (high-mining states tend to be adjacent to other high-mining states), but the result falls just below the conventional statistical significance threshold of $\alpha = 0.05$. This means we cannot conclusively reject the null hypothesis of spatial randomness at the state level, though the positive direction of the statistic is consistent with coordinated cross-border criminal networks.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_morans_i.png}
\caption{Global Moran's I analysis. Left: Moran scatter plot ($I = 0.121$, $p = 0.108$); the positive regression slope indicates mild spatial clustering. Right: state-level choropleth of total mining records showing the Gangetic corridor and Central India plateau as the primary concentration zones.}
\label{fig:morans}
\end{figure}

\textbf{LISA Hotspot Analysis.} Local Indicators of Spatial Association (LISA) decompose the global Moran's I into state-level contributions, allowing us to identify specific geographic hotspots and coldspots. Under this method, states are classified into four quadrant types: High-High (HH, a high-mining state surrounded by other high-mining states), Low-Low (LL), High-Low (HL, an isolated high-mining state surrounded by low-mining neighbors), and Low-High (LH).

At $p < 0.05$ (under 999 permutations), the following classifications emerge:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{HH hotspot states:} Goa, Gujarat, Madhya Pradesh, and Rajasthan: a geographically contiguous belt indicating a cross-border enforcement problem. These states share rivers (most importantly the Chambal) and likely share criminal networks.
    \item \textbf{HL spatial outlier:} Assam, a high-mining state isolated from the main concentration belt, suggesting locally specific drivers (likely the Brahmaputra river and its tributaries) rather than network spillovers from neighboring states.
    \item \textbf{LH spatial outliers:} Chhattisgarh, Dadra and Nagar Haveli, and Jharkhand: low-mining states surrounded by relatively higher-mining neighbors.
    \item \textbf{LL coldspots:} Andaman and Nicobar Islands and Lakshadweep, remote island territories with negligible mining activity.
\end{itemize}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.55\textwidth]{fig_lisa_hotspots.png}
\caption{LISA hotspot map ($p < 0.05$, 999 permutations). The red High-High cluster (Goa, Gujarat, Madhya Pradesh, Rajasthan) forms a geographically contiguous belt sharing the Chambal and Narmada river systems. The orange High-Low outlier (Assam) reflects locally specific Brahmaputra basin drivers. Teal Low-High outliers (Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand) sit adjacent to higher-mining neighbours.}
\label{fig:lisa}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Spatial Regression Models.} We estimated three regression models to assess whether crime rate, PM2.5 levels, and PM10 levels predict state-level mining intensity:

An \textit{Ordinary Least Squares} (OLS) model serves as the baseline: it estimates the linear relationship between mining intensity (log-transformed) and the predictors without accounting for the geographic relationships between states. The OLS model achieves $R^2 = 0.158$, with PM10 showing the largest coefficient ($\beta = +1.17$, $p = 0.123$), though no predictor reaches individual statistical significance. The low $R^2$ indicates that state-level environmental and crime variables explain only a modest fraction of the variance in mining intensity. This is not surprising: geography (which rivers a state contains) and political economy (which networks have entrenched themselves) are likely more important determinants.

To test whether spatial dependence among states biases the OLS estimates, we computed Lagrange Multiplier tests for spatial lag dependence and spatial error dependence. Neither test reaches significance ($p = 0.98$ for the lag test, $p = 0.74$ for the error test), so OLS is not strongly violated. We nevertheless estimate a \textit{Spatial Lag Model} (SLM), which adds a term for the weighted average of neighboring states' mining intensity as a predictor, and a \textit{Spatial Error Model} (SEM), which assumes spatial correlation enters through the error term (unexplained variation) rather than the mean. Neither model improves substantially on OLS (AIC of 97.97 and 95.81 respectively, versus 95.97 for OLS), consistent with the non-significant Moran's I.

\textbf{Geographically Weighted Regression (GWR).} Standard regression assumes that the relationship between predictors and outcomes is the same everywhere. Geographically Weighted Regression relaxes this assumption by allowing regression coefficients to vary across space: at each location, a separate regression is estimated using a locally weighted subset of nearby observations, where closer observations receive higher weights. This produces a map of locally varying coefficients rather than a single global estimate.

We applied GWR to the relationship between groundwater quality (log-transformed Total Dissolved Solids, TDS) at 7,190 WRIS monitoring stations and each station's distance to the nearest confirmed mining point. A negative local GWR coefficient on this distance variable means that closer proximity to mining is associated with higher TDS: the expected contamination signal. Globally, the GWR achieves $R^2 = 0.545$, a substantial improvement over OLS, indicating that the relationship between mining proximity and water quality is highly spatially heterogeneous.

The state-level breakdown of local GWR coefficients reveals striking regional variation. In Bihar, 100\% of monitoring stations show negative coefficients (closer to mining = worse water quality), with a median $\beta = -0.346$. In West Bengal, 78\% of stations show negative coefficients (median $\beta = -0.505$). In Madhya Pradesh, only 39\% show negative coefficients (median $\beta = +0.215$), suggesting that the naturally mineral-rich hard-rock geology of the Chambal basin masks the contamination signal from mining.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_gwr_coefficients.png}
\caption{GWR local coefficients for distance-to-nearest-mining-site predicting log(TDS) at 7,190 WRIS groundwater stations. Left: negative (blue/dark) values indicate that proximity to mining correlates with elevated dissolved solids; the signal is concentrated in the Gangetic corridor (Bihar, West Bengal). Right: local $R^2$ surface, confirming that the mining-water quality relationship is strongest along the Ganga and its tributaries.}
\label{fig:gwr}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Random Forest Classification.} To identify which spatial and environmental features are most predictive of whether a location is a mining site (as opposed to a random background location within India), we trained a Random Forest classifier (an ensemble of many individual decision trees that vote on the classification of each input). Using 373 confirmed mining points and 357 randomly generated background points within India's boundaries as training data, the model achieves a 5-fold cross-validated ROC-AUC (Receiver Operating Characteristic Area Under the Curve, a standard measure of classifier performance ranging from 0.5 for random chance to 1.0 for perfect classification) of $0.965 \pm 0.014$. The feature importance ranking (measured by permutation importance, which assesses how much model performance degrades when each feature is randomly shuffled) places longitude and latitude first and second (confirming the strong geographic patterning evident in the KDE maps), followed by crime rate (third), PM10 (fourth), and PM2.5 (fifth). The fact that crime rate ranks ahead of both air quality variables is notable: it suggests that the enforcement environment is at least as strong a co-determinant of mining location as environmental conditions.

