\subsubsection{Construction Demand and Causal Inference (construction\_mining\_analysis.ipynb)}
\label{sec:construction}

This section asks the most policy-relevant question in our analysis: did the 2015 launch of the PMAY-U housing scheme cause an increase in illegal sand mining, or is the observed temporal correlation driven by confounding factors (for example, improved data collection infrastructure after 2015)?

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\textwidth]{fig_spatial_clusters.png}
\caption{Side-by-side state choropleths of PMAY houses sanctioned (left) and total mining incidents (right). States with large PMAY allocations overlap substantially with high-mining states. Bivariate Moran's I $= +0.033$ ($p = 0.417$), indicating spatial co-clustering that falls just short of conventional significance.}
\label{fig:spatial_clusters}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Temporal Analysis.} The mining time series shows a clear structural break around 2015. Pre-2015 annual averages are 13 incidents per year; post-2015 averages are 191 incidents per year. A Mann-Whitney U test (a non-parametric test that compares the medians of two distributions without assuming they are normally distributed) confirms this difference is statistically significant ($p = 0.0011$). However, a Chow test for a structural break (a test that asks whether the regression slope and intercept change significantly at a specified breakpoint) does not reach significance at the 2015 breakpoint ($F = 0.44$, $p = 0.655$), suggesting that while levels increased, the rate of change was already trending upward before 2015.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_temporal_surge.png}
\caption{Annual mining incident counts (left) and year-on-year growth rates post-2015 (right). Pre-PMAY average: 13 incidents/year (blue). Post-PMAY average: 191 incidents/year (red), a 14.7$\times$ surge. The Chow test ($p = 0.655$) does not find a discrete structural break, suggesting a gradual acceleration rather than an abrupt step change at the policy launch.}
\label{fig:temporal}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Difference-in-Differences.} To move closer to a causal claim, we implement a Difference-in-Differences (DiD) design. This method compares the change in outcomes before and after a policy intervention in a ``treated'' group (states with high PMAY-U allocations) against the change in a ``control'' group (states with low allocations), under the assumption that both groups would have followed parallel trends in the absence of the program. Treatment is defined as being above the median in PMAY houses sanctioned per state.

We estimate a Two-Way Fixed Effects (TWFE) DiD model, which simultaneously controls for time-invariant differences between states (state fixed effects) and common time trends across all states (year fixed effects), thereby isolating the treatment effect:

\begin{equation}
\log(\text{mining}_{it}) = \beta \cdot (\text{Treated}_i \times \text{Post}_t) + \alpha_i + \gamma_t + \varepsilon_{it}
\end{equation}

where $\alpha_i$ represents state fixed effects and $\gamma_t$ represents year fixed effects. The estimated DiD coefficient is $\hat{\beta} = +1.057$ ($p < 0.001$, 95\% CI: $[0.855, 1.259]$), implying that high-PMAY states experienced 187.7\% more mining post-2015 than low-PMAY states, relative to their pre-2015 difference. An event study design (which estimates separate treatment effects for each year relative to the PMAY launch) shows no significant pre-2015 differential trends between treated and control states ($F = 0.406$, $p = 0.805$), supporting the parallel trends assumption.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_event_study.png}
\caption{Event study DiD: estimated treatment effects for high-PMAY versus low-PMAY states by year relative to the 2015 PMAY launch (year 0, red dashed line). Pre-period coefficients (green background) are flat and statistically indistinguishable from zero ($F = 0.406$, $p = 0.805$), directly supporting the parallel trends assumption. Post-period coefficients (pink background) rise monotonically, reaching $+1.5$ log-mining units by year $+9$.}
\label{fig:eventstudy}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Instrumental Variables (2SLS).} A key concern with the DiD design is reverse causality or selection: perhaps states that were already heavily mined received less PMAY allocation because of political dynamics, or mining-intensive states had pre-existing construction industries that attracted more PMAY investment. To address this, we estimate a Two-Stage Least Squares (2SLS) model using the central government's financial assistance to each state (in crore rupees) as an instrument for PMAY construction scale.

An instrument is valid if it (1) strongly predicts the treatment variable (PMAY scale) and (2) affects the outcome (mining) only through its effect on the treatment, not through any independent channel. Central assistance is determined by a national-level formula based on urban population and housing deficit rather than by local mining conditions, making it plausibly exogenous to state-level mining propensity. The first stage confirms instrument strength ($F = 16.40$, $p < 0.001$). The 2SLS estimate of the PMAY effect on mining ($\hat{\beta}_{2SLS} = +0.631$) is slightly larger than the OLS estimate ($\hat{\beta}_{OLS} = +0.589$), with the Hausman difference ($+0.042$) suggesting that OLS may slightly underestimate the true causal effect, consistent with attenuation bias from measurement error in PMAY allocation figures.

\textbf{Synthetic Control for Uttar Pradesh.} As a complementary causal analysis, we construct a ``synthetic Uttar Pradesh'' (UP), a weighted combination of donor states that best matches UP's pre-2015 mining trajectory, and compare actual post-2015 UP mining against this counterfactual. The pre-period fit is good (Root Mean Square Error $= 0.12$), with the synthetic control drawing weights primarily from an unnamed central Indian state (72.4\%), Arunachal Pradesh (11.7\%), Madhya Pradesh (9.9\%), and Maharashtra (6.0\%). Post-2015, actual UP mining diverges from the synthetic control by an average of $+0.948$ log-mining units, translating to 158\% excess mining relative to the counterfactual, consistent with the DiD estimate.

