\subsubsection{NLP-Based Classification of Illegality (classification.ipynb)}
\label{sec:classification}

A core analytical challenge is that not every observed mining incident in the India Sand Watch data is unambiguously illegal. Some observations may describe permitted operations incorrectly identified as illegal; others may describe genuinely illegal mining without using legal terminology. We designed a rule-based scoring system to classify field observations by their probability of reflecting illegal activity, drawing on patterns derived from the court document corpus.

\textbf{Feature Engineering.} For each of the 375 field observations, we extract three classes of signals from the free-text ``Notes from the location'' field:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textit{Operational signals:} Binary indicators for whether the text mentions machinery (JCBs, excavators, pumps) and transport vehicles (trucks, tractors, trolleys). These are weighted most heavily (3 points each) because large-scale mechanized extraction is definitionally inconsistent with small-scale permitted mining under the Mines and Minerals (Development and Regulation) Act.
    \item \textit{Risk signals:} Binary indicators for nighttime activity, high-volume extraction, and repeated/daily operations (1 to 1.5 points each). These amplify the illegality score when present alongside operational signals.
    \item \textit{Legal signals:} Pattern matching against nine regular expression (text pattern) rules derived from legal language found in court documents: references to illegal mining, unauthorized operations, lack of environmental clearance, lease violations, and excess extraction (2 points each match).
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Classification Thresholds.} Observations scoring above the 80th percentile of the total score distribution are classified as \textit{highly likely illegal}; those between the 50th and 80th percentiles are classified as \textit{possibly illegal}. Of 375 observations, 121 (32\%) are classified as highly likely illegal and 254 (68\%) as possibly illegal.

\textbf{Findings.} The states with the highest absolute counts of highly likely illegal observations are Uttar Pradesh (32), Madhya Pradesh (30), West Bengal (25), and Bihar (19). When normalized by total observations per state, the highest proportions are in Goa (100\%), Himachal Pradesh (100\%), Rajasthan (87.5\%), and Madhya Pradesh (69.8\%). Bihar has the lowest proportion (9.8\%) despite having the most total field observations, which is consistent with its large volume of lower-intensity, possibly artisanal (small-scale, manual) extraction that does not trigger the operational machinery signals.

\textbf{Enforcement Gap Analysis.} We compute, for each state, the gap between the rate of highly likely illegal observations and the rate of court-recognized illegal cases. States where observed illegal mining far exceeds court cases are those where enforcement is most severely lagging behind documented criminal activity. Himachal Pradesh, West Bengal, and Jharkhand show the largest positive gaps (more observed illegal activity than court activity), while Haryana and Karnataka show negative gaps (more court cases than field observations, possibly reflecting stronger proactive enforcement). This gap analysis directly identifies the states where strengthened enforcement would have the highest expected impact.

\subsubsection{Water Quality: Ganga and Sangam Sensor Analysis (spatial\_analysis.ipynb, Section 11)}

Two continuous monitoring sensors deployed at Prayagraj, Uttar Pradesh captured approximately 99,000 combined one-minute water quality readings between January 2019 and February 2020. We treat these sensors as a case study in what longitudinal, high-resolution environmental monitoring of mining impacts could look like at scale.

