\end{figure}

\textbf{Seasonal Patterns.} Our primary hypothesis is that river conductivity (a measure of dissolved mineral content and a proxy for riverbed disturbance) should peak during the dry season (November through February) when river levels fall, making riverbed sand accessible and allowing mineral-rich porewater to concentrate. Excluding the Kumbh Mela period (a major religious gathering that introduced confounding through exceptional crowd and sanitation effects), dry-season Ganga conductivity averages 478 µS/cm versus 276 µS/cm during the monsoon (June through September), a 1.73$\times$ ratio with Cohen's $d = 1.78$ (a very large effect by conventional standards) and a Mann-Whitney $p < 0.001$. A monthly Spearman correlation between conductivity and mining incidents shows $\rho = 0.536$ ($p = 0.089$), not quite reaching conventional significance in a dataset of only 12 monthly observations, but in the expected direction.

